<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ukraine</h1><p class="st-meta">1991 CE – present · capital Kyiv</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Eastern European republic independent since 1991 under the 1996 constitution, shaped by the Orange Revolution, Euromaidan and war with Russia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ukraine had been the Ukrainian SSR, a constituent republic of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> since 1922. Its Supreme Soviet adopted the Declaration of State <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a> on 16 July 1990, and after a failed coup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> independence was proclaimed on 24 August 1991. A referendum on 1 December approved it with 92% of the electorate. The first president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonid-kravchuk">Leonid Kravchuk</a>, signed the Belavezha Accords, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> formally ended on 26 December.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verkhovna-rada">Verkhovna Rada</a> adopted the present constitution on 28 June 1996, establishing a semi-presidential republic. During the transition to a market economy Ukraine lost 60% of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a> between 1991 and 1999, with hyperinflation that peaked at 10,000% in 1993, and mass privatisation created powerful oligarchs. Politics divided over relations with the West and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. After Kravchuk and Leonid Kuchma, Yushchenko was elected president after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-revolution">Orange Revolution</a> of 2004, when tens of thousands protested election rigging in favour of Yanukovych, who later also held the presidency.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Protests on the Euromaidan began when Yanukovych refused to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> association agreement, and on 21 February 2014 he fled and was removed by parliament. In late February and March 2014 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, and a war began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donbas">Donbas</a>. Russia launched a full-scale invasion on 24 February 2022, and Ukraine was granted candidate status for the European Union on 23 June 2022.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ukraine.jpg" alt="Ukraine" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Government of Ukraine · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ukraine.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1991 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kyiv</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Leonid Kravchuk, Leonid Kuchma, Yushchenko, Yanukovych</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox, Ukrainian Greek Catholic, Protestant</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ukrainian, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=ukraine" aria-label="Ukraine on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Ukraine lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5029 5813L5029 5813M5029 5813L5366 5342"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5029 5813h0M5366 5342h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5029 5813h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5029 5813h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kyiv. <a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=ukraine">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
